---
title: Bo ssam
servings: 10
yield: 10 to 12 servings
tags: [korean, pork, make-ahead, weekend]
cuisine: korean
time: 29 hours 30 minutes
source: Momofuku Bossam (Korean Slow Cooked Pork Roast) by Nagi Maehashi, RecipeTin Eats, after David Chang's Momofuku, https://www.recipetineats.com/momofuku-bossam-korean-slow-cooked-pork/
---

== Cure ==

Mix the @sugar{110%g} and @kosher salt{115%g}. Pat the
@pork shoulder{3.5-4%kg}(bone in, skinless) dry and rub the mixture all over.
Discard what does not stick. Set it in a #glass baking dish{}, cover loosely,
and refrigerate ~{24-36%hours}.

== Roast ==

Heat the oven to 150°C (300°F) with a rack in the middle.

Pour off the liquid the pork has given up, or move it to a clean
#roasting pan{}. Do not rinse it.

Roast uncovered ~{5%hours}, basting with the pan juices every hour, until it
pulls apart easily with two forks.

Rest it ~{10%minutes}, or up to an hour loosely covered with foil.

== Crust ==

Turn the oven up to 260°C (500°F). Mix the @light brown sugar{7%tbsp} with
@kosher salt{1%tbsp}.

Baste the pork once more, then press the sugar mixture evenly over the top and
sides. Roast ~{10%minutes}, checking from 5 minutes, until deep golden and
bubbling. Cover any spots that start to blacken with a scrap of foil.

Move the pork to a platter. Leave the pan juices behind; the cure makes them
too salty to use.

== Ginger scallion sauce ==

Mix the @scallions{2 1/2%cups}(thinly sliced, about 5 to 6),
@ginger{1/2%cup}(finely grated), @canola oil{1/3%cup},
@light soy sauce{1 1/2%tsp}, @sherry vinegar{1%tsp} and
@kosher salt{1/2%tsp}. Leave ~{20%minutes}, then taste for salt.

== Ssam sauce ==

Stir together the @ssamjang{2%tbsp}, @gochujang{1%tbsp},
@sherry vinegar{5%tsp}, @canola oil{2%tbsp}, @water{1%tbsp} and
@sugar{1%tsp}. Stir again before serving; the oil separates.

== To serve ==

Set out the pork with both sauces, the @butter lettuce{4-5%heads}(leaves separated),
@white rice{3%cups}(cooked), @kimchi{500%g} and
@?oysters{12}(raw, in the shell). Pull the pork apart at the table and wrap it
in lettuce leaves with rice and sauce.

> Cure it for at least a full day: that is what seasons the meat right through.
